  "articleSummary": "**Summary of \"No Fate But What We Bill\"**\n\nThis is an analytical security-forecast essay arguing that the agentic AI threat is not hype but arrives through two ordinary, compounding curves rather than any single breakthrough: agent *endurance* (METR's finding that autonomous task length doubles every ~4–7 months) and orchestration *breadth* (one coordinator running dozens of sub-agents, exemplified by Anthropic's GTG-1002 disclosure where Claude Code orchestrated espionage across ~30 targets). The threat emerges from the product of these curves—duration × breadth—while security review scales only linearly. A key economic argument is \"LLMjacking\": attackers using stolen cloud credentials mean the victim pays the compute bill, severing the industry's assumption that the capability user is the payer, so pricing/access gates fail against a tenant rather than a customer. Cheap per-attempt swarm economics mean defenders' per-attempt failure comfort is statistically wrong (1 − (1−p)^n math). The tone is a pointed, well-written analysis/rant aimed at engineering and security teams skeptical of agentic risk, sustained by a Terminator/Skynet framing device: the robot is \"an API call,\" and \"absolutely will not stop\" now means \"absolutely will not stop billing.\"",
